---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
    <head>
        <meta charset="UTF-8">
        <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
        <title>Yellow-eyed Penguin</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <header class="header">
            <h1>Yellow-eyed Penguin</h1>
        </header>
        <Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>

                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Megadyptes antipodes</li>
                        <li>Size: Approximately 65-70 cm (26-28 inches) tall, weighing 5-8 kg (11-17.6 lbs).</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Found along the southeastern coast of New Zealand's South Island, Stewart Island/Rakiura, and several offshore islands.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Endangered</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>The Yellow-eyed Penguin's diet consists mainly of fish, particularly blue cod, red cod, and opalfish, as well as squid. They are known to forage relatively far offshore.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>Yellow-eyed Penguins typically nest as solitary pairs or in loosely scattered groups, often hidden in coastal forests or scrub. They are unusual among penguins for their dispersed nesting habits. They form monogamous pairs and have a relatively long breeding season. Both parents are involved in incubating the eggs and caring for the chicks.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>They are easily recognized by the distinctive band of yellow feathers that runs around their eyes and joins at the back of their head.</li>
                        <li>Yellow-eyed Penguins are one of the rarest penguin species in the world.</li>
                        <li>They face numerous threats, including habitat loss, introduced predators, climate change, and disturbance from human activities.</li>
                        <li>Conservation efforts are intensive and include habitat restoration, predator control, and managing human impacts.</li>
                        <li>They are also known as Hoiho in Māori.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
    </body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>